{
  "slice": "PLAY-01 — native-HLS Safari via safaridriver",
  "date": "2026-09-09",
  "rows": {
    "PLAY-01": "UNVERIFIED -> PASS (Chromium: A07/A40; native-HLS Safari 26.5 via safaridriver: this slice)"
  },
  "mechanism": {
    "used": "(c) a WebDriver-injected script that removes both MSE spellings from the document, followed by an in-app (soft) navigation to the watch page",
    "script": "delete window.MediaSource; delete window.ManagedMediaSource",
    "why": "lib/player-engine.ts gates the hls-js engine on probeSupport().mseSupported = \"MediaSource\" in window || \"ManagedMediaSource\" in window. Safari 26.5 has BOTH, so hls.js wins selection; with both absent the next candidate is native-hls, whose gate is video.canPlayType(\"application/vnd.apple.mpegurl\") — \"maybe\" in Safari.",
    "options_not_used": {
      "(a) a shipped or documented override": "NONE EXISTS. Selection in lib/player-engine.ts is capability-only: no query parameter, no instance setting, no admin knob, no build flag names an engine.",
      "(b) a lab-only flag the player already reads": "none — the same absence as (a).",
      "(d) a lab-only NEXT_PUBLIC_PLAYER_ENGINE_ORDER override": "not needed, and therefore not added: (c) worked, and it leaves the shipped default order untouched, which (d) would not."
    },
    "limitation": "safaridriver exposes no pre-document-script hook (no Page.addScriptToEvaluateOnNewDocument equivalent), so the deletion cannot happen before the page's own scripts run. It happens before the PLAYER mounts, which is what selection reads: the home feed is loaded, both globals are deleted, and the watch page is reached by clicking a Next.js <Link> — one document, no reload. Every measurement below asserts on the watch page that MediaSource and ManagedMediaSource are still absent; a run that arrived as a NEW document (a click that hard-navigated before hydration) is discarded and retried, because that run would have taken the hls.js path while claiming not to.",
    "honesty_caveat": "The shipped default engine order WAS exercised — nothing in the app was changed to reach native-hls — but the browser was. No shipped Apple browser reaches this engine today: hls.js 1.7.1 prefers ManagedMediaSource (getMediaSource() takes it over MediaSource), so iOS Safari, which exposes ManagedMediaSource, answers isSupported() true as well. native-hls is therefore reachable in production only where hls.js's own isSupported() declines at import time. That makes this a measurement of a latent path, and the register cell says so."
  },
  "lab": {
    "origin": "http://127.0.0.1:8100 — one origin, a pipe-only proxy splitting deploy/Caddyfile's own @api path set to core and everything else to the frontend, Accept-Encoding dropped, bodies never transformed. vidra_refresh is SameSite=Lax with Path=/api/v1/auth, so one origin is not a convenience.",
    "lab_only_exception": "an HLS-only bandwidth shaper in that proxy, armed by writing a bytes-per-second number to a file. It paces writes and rewrites no byte; it exists because a loopback origin never gives Safari's ABR a reason to move.",
    "core": "two processes built from vidra-core main (schema 146, migrated 0 -> 146 from an EMPTY initdb, dirty=false): VIDRA_ROLE=api on 127.0.0.1:8188 and a separate VIDRA_ROLE=worker",
